4|0@UFOs^1440523654#cuf9tm8:CaerBannog$:&gt; Project Hessdalen uses spectral photography Hessdalen Project hasn't verified ET, though. It has captured bizarre plasmas &amp; EM fields. If it had pinged a structured craft and the spectra showed a metal hull with various alloys, then you'd have a point, but they found plasma. Irrespective of any of these claims and events, that there is no evidence ET is visiting this planet. My words were "There is zero evidence of ETs visiting Earth. ... testable, verifiable evidence." We can argue over semantics, but the point is really *testable* in the scientific sense, and this is what would be needed to demonstrate once and for all that ET is visiting the planet. We haven't got that. This is what Neil Degrasse Tyson meant about the alien ashtray. Until we have that, there's not even a discussion to be had for anyone outside this field. &gt;This is a question of technology and application of science, not the fundamental falsifiability of the ETH as an explanation for the phenomenon. I disagree that the ETH is unfalsifiable, but that argument isn't anything to do with my point, which is simply that right now we do not have any evidence ET is visiting this planet. Period. That is all I'm saying, and I make it to address wild assumptions that are made by UFO enthusiasts, this extensive lore about ETs and what they're doing here, it is a mythos that has no right to exist. It harms UFO research. I don't want to argue about verifiable vs verified or falsifiable. That's semantic stuff and to me is putting the cart before the horse. Without something to measure and learn from, it's just talking about systems of belief, and while that discussion is worth having, it is not what I'm driving at here. What I'm saying is, in 70 years UFOlogy has failed to establish anything substantive, we know little or nothing about what anomalous aerial phenomena really are. From that, to have any assumptions about what UFOs might be in the face of that failure is the wrong approach. Speculation is fine, belief is not. You know I do not dispute the strangeness of truly anomalous UFO events, I'm just stating the heresy that there is no evidence ET is visiting the Earth. There is circumstantial evidence based on reports, but because of distortion, human perceptual problems and all the other cognitive errors we face (including, perhaps, deliberate deception on the part of the force responsible for the phenomenon), it isn't reliable. This is the unpalatable truth of UFOlogy. It's not going away. 
2|0@UFOs^1440425331#cudvwwx:laowhoo$:The moment you admit the possibility that there have been encounters with things not of our 4-D earth, you also quickly realize that there's not likely to have been any beginning point for this that we could ever know. The only real time constraint that can be placed upon "them" would be the age of the planet. Could activity have increased during the Cold War? Sure, but this as any kind of "nuclear beginning" of the phenom is both illogical and highly ET-based. Once you let "them" in as a possibility you can never not always assume that they've just always been around (occasionally dipping their multi-dimensional toes into our 4-D waters). Went on a Kubrick kick and watched A Life in Pictures https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=h-JEAfLwFA8 For 2001 they said they were after a multi-dimensional effect vis a vis super-luminal travel (2 UFO defaults), but I was hoping they'd discuss the monolith more.
