2|0@UFOs^1440439952#cue4xmp:UniversalChairs$:Actually, yes, there are a great many factors a person can look at for trying to determine the likelihood. They are the same factors as used in [the Drake Equation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drake_equation), which is the thing most people cite when they say that aliens likely do exist somewhere. What it basically boils down to is that there are 100 billion stars in our galaxy, there are about 40 billion earth-sized planets orbiting what we consider "habitable zones" in our galaxy, and so even if life was exceedingly rare (let's say life only occurs in 0.01% of planets where life *could* exist) **then we would still have 400 million planets with life on them!** Now, really think about how Earth is only one out of those 40 billion earth-like planets. And let's even take our generous estimation that we could have 400 million planets with life on them. We would still only be one out of 400 million planets that interstellar aliens would have any interest in visiting. One out of 400 million. We ourselves are still so primitive that we can't even visit even ONE other Earth-like planet. Why should we be so special that superior races would take interest in us over any of the other hundreds of millions of earth-like planets? Now, like you, I want to believe that aliens have visited this planet. That would be awesome. But unlike you, I don't feel that I've seen anything I could call evidence of it.
